Our 7-feature ensemble outperforms all individual baselines by 6--11\,pp in 3-class F1.
Multi-detector ensembles achieve moderately higher 3-class F1 (0.785--0.795 vs.\ 0.774) with indistinguishable binary performance ($\leq$0.3\,pp), but require 2.4$\times$ longer inference and 3.2$\times$ more GPU memory (two models, 1.4B parameters vs.\ our single 410M reference).


\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

If depth fingerprinting reliably identifies recursive text, can it improve data curation?
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) yields two conclusions.
At equal sample count ($n$\,=\,3{,}951), depth-aware selection is practically equivalent to random subsampling (ppl 2.387 vs.\ 2.367; TOST $\pm$2\% relative margin, $p = 1.1 \times 10^{-4}$).
However, data quantity dominates: the full dataset ($n$\,=\,12{,}299, ppl\,=\,2.335) yields significantly lower perplexity than any depth-filtered subset, and TOST rejects equivalence at the same margin (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).
Depth composition per se does not determine training utility at fixed sample count.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced generation depth classification and documented a differential depth response across eight base-model generators from five families: recursive generation is consistent with a systematic location shift in the token-level NLL distribution that preserves distributional shape, and this structured drift is detectable with a lightweight 7-feature ensemble (binary F1\,=\,0.764 on cross-model chains, 0.694 under LOGO; 3-class: 0.809 and 0.762, respectively).
A TOST equivalence test confirms that depth-aware data curation yields training utility equivalent to random subsampling ($\pm$2\%, $p < 0.001$) within chains of $\leq$4 generations as measured by perplexity, indicating that detectability does not entail downstream perplexity degradation.

Two boundaries define the scope: instruction tuning compresses the distributional variation that fingerprinting exploits, and frontier-scale models (70B+) may further compress feature margins.
All code, data, and trained models will be released upon publication.

\section*{Ethics Statement}
\label{sec:ethics}

All texts analyzed in this work are LLM-generated from publicly available Wikipedia seeds; no personal or sensitive data is involved.
Our method estimates the generation depth of a text passage (how many recursive generation steps it has undergone), not the identity of its author.
Individual authorship attribution is outside both the scope and the capability of this approach; privacy implications are minimal.
We acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be used to selectively suppress machine-generated text in contexts where such content is legitimate (e.g., accessibility tools, assistive writing).
We encourage deployment in data governance pipelines where provenance transparency, rather than content removal, is the objective.

\paragraph{Licenses.}
All generator models are publicly available under permissive licenses (Apache 2.0 for Pythia, OLMo, and Mistral; Qwen License for Qwen3; Llama Community License for Llama 3.2).
Wikipedia seed passages are distributed under CC BY-SA 3.0.

\paragraph{Compute budget.}
All experiments were conducted on 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32\,GB each), totaling approximately 62 GPU-hours.

\paragraph{Software.}
Experiments used Python 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.10.0 (CUDA 12.8), Transformers 4.57.6, XGBoost 3.2.0, scikit-learn 1.8.0, NumPy 2.2.6, pandas 3.0.2, and SciPy 1.17.1.

